'''Glasgow North East''' is a [[burgh constituency]] of the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom]] (at [[Palace of Westminster|Westminster]]). It was first contested at the [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005 general election]]. The current [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) is [[Anne McLaughlin]] of the [[Scottish National Party|SNP]] who gained the seat from [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]]'s [[Paul Sweeney]] at the [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general election]].

==History==
From the seat's creation until 2009, the constituency was represented by [[Michael Martin, Baron Martin of Springburn|Michael Martin]], previously MP for [[Glasgow Springburn (UK Parliament constituency)|Glasgow Springburn]] from 1979. Martin was elected [[Speaker of the House of Commons (United Kingdom)|Speaker of the House of Commons]] in October 2000, but in May 2009 he announced that he would be resigning as Speaker on 21 June 2009 because of his perceived role in the [[United Kingdom parliamentary expenses scandal|MPs' expenses controversy]]. He was the first Speaker in 300 years to be forced out of office by a motion of no confidence.<ref name="LotBNote">{{cite web|url=http://lordsoftheblog.net/2009/05/19/a-note-on-the-speakership/|title=A note on the Speakership|publisher=Hansard Society|date=21 October 2009|work=Lords of the Blog|access-date=21 October 2009|archive-date=1 July 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090701113018/http://lordsoftheblog.net/2009/05/19/a-note-on-the-speakership/|url-status=live}}</ref> He also resigned as an MP the following day, resulting in [[2009 Glasgow North East by-election|a by-election]] on 12 November 2009, which was won by [[Willie Bain]] of the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] with 59% of the vote.

Bain retained the seat the following year at the [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010 UK general election]], but was defeated by Anne McLaughlin of the SNP in [[2015 United Kingdom general election|2015]]. The seat was regained by Labour's candidate [[Paul Sweeney]] at the [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017 snap general election]], only to be regained by McLaughlin at the [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general election]].

==Boundaries==
{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Glasgow North East (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame=yes|text=Map of current boundaries}}
The constituency contains two [[Glasgow City Council]] wards in full: [[Dennistoun (ward)|Dennistoun]] and [[Springburn/Robroyston (ward)|Springburn & Robroyston]]; and also partially covers [[Canal (ward)|Canal]], [[Calton (ward)|Calton]], [[East Centre (Glasgow ward)|East Centre]] and [[North East (Glasgow ward)|North East]] wards.

The constituency partially overlaps with two [[Scottish Parliament]] seats: [[Glasgow Maryhill and Springburn (Scottish Parliament constituency)|Glasgow Maryhill and Springburn]] and [[Glasgow Provan (Scottish Parliament constituency)|Glasgow Provan]].

Glasgow North East is one of seven constituencies covering the [[Glasgow City council area]]. All are entirely within the council area.

Prior to the 2005 general election, the city area was covered by ten constituencies, two of which straddled the boundaries of other council areas. The North East constituency includes most of the former [[Glasgow Springburn (UK Parliament constituency)|Glasgow Springburn]] constituency and a small part of the former [[Glasgow Maryhill (UK Parliament constituency)|Glasgow Maryhill]] constituency.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bcomm-scotland.gov.uk/5th%20report/index.htm |title=Fifth Periodical Review |website=[[Boundary Commission for Scotland]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071009160559fw_/http://www.bcomm-scotland.gov.uk/5th%20report/index.htm |archive-date=9 October 2007}}</ref>

== Constituency profile ==
The population of the constituency was 88,156 at the time of the [[2011 UK Census]]. It comprises the communities of [[Ruchill]], [[Hamiltonhill]], [[Possilpark]], [[Port Dundas]], [[Sighthill, Glasgow|Sighthill]], [[Lambhill]], [[Colston, Glasgow|Colston]], [[Milton, Glasgow|Milton]], [[Springburn]], [[Royston, Glasgow|Royston]], [[Balornock]], [[Barmulloch]], [[Blackhill, Glasgow|Blackhill]], [[Blochairn]], [[Dennistoun]], [[Germiston, Glasgow|Germiston]], [[Haghill]], [[Carntyne]], [[Robroyston]], [[Provanmill]], [[Riddrie]], [[Hogganfield]], [[Millerston]] and [[Ruchazie]].

===Voting pattern===
Glasgow North East and its predecessor constituencies had been represented by MPs from the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] with large majorities from the [[1935 United Kingdom general election|1935 general election]] until [[2015 United Kingdom general election|2015]], when the seat was gained by the [[Scottish National Party|SNP]] during their landslide victory on the largest swing recorded at the general election that year of 39.3% from Labour to SNP. At the following election [[2017 United Kingdom general election|held just two years later]], the seat was regained on a 12% swing by Labour's [[Paul Sweeney]] with a narrow majority of 242 votes (0.7%). However, the SNP regained the constituency with a marginal majority of 7% in 2019.

According to the ''[[British Election Study]]'', it is the most [[Left-wing politics|left-wing]] seat in the country.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-30129990|title=The strange truth about how and why we vote|first=Brian|last=Wheeler|work=BBC News |date=1 December 2014|access-date=21 June 2018|archive-date=7 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171207174146/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-30129990|url-status=live}}</ref>

It had the lowest turnout of any seat at the [[2017 United Kingdom general election]].

