'''North Ayrshire and Arran''' is a [[United Kingdom constituencies|constituency]] of the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|British House of Commons]], located in the south-west of [[Scotland]] within the [[North Ayrshire]] council area. It elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) at least once every five years using the [[first-past-the-post voting]] system of voting.

Once a longtime [[Scottish Conservative Party|Conservative]] seat, the area had been represented by [[Scottish Labour Party|Labour]] MPs from 1987 until 2015. [[Patricia Gibson]], has held the seat as an [[Scottish National Party|SNP]] member since 2015.

It contains the towns of [[Largs]], [[Fairlie, North Ayrshire|Fairlie]] and [[West Kilbride]] to the north, as well as the towns of [[Ardrossan]], [[Kilbirnie]], the [[Garnock Valley]], [[Kilwinning]], [[Saltcoats]] and [[Stevenston]] to the south. The [[Isle of Arran]] and [[Great Cumbrae]] are also within the constituency.

==Boundaries==
{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/North Ayrshire and Arran (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame=yes|frame-height=250|text=Map of current boundaries}}
The constituency is entirely within the [[North Ayrshire]] council area. Part of the same council area is covered by the [[Central Ayrshire (UK Parliament constituency)|Central Ayrshire]] constituency.

The boundaries of North Ayrshire and Arran extend to, and include, [[Skelmorlie]] in the north of the council area, [[Irvine, North Ayrshire|Irvine]] in the south, [[Beith]] in the east, and the islands of [[Isle of Arran|Arran]] and [[Cumbrae]] in the west.

==Constituency profile==
[[File:Ardrossan Scotland.jpg|thumb|A view of Ardrossan]]
The seat takes in many towns and villages which have some very affluent areas and more working class areas, The towns of [[Skelmorlie]], [[Largs]], [[Fairlie, North Ayrshire|Fairlie]] and [[West Kilbride]] in the north are affluent commuter towns, while [[Ardrossan]], [[Saltcoats]], and [[Stevenston]] in the south are rather more industrial,  However, regeneration is taking place at Ardrossan [[Harbour]] and there has been a rapid increase in new build housing in recent years. Inland, the seat takes in the Garnock valley towns of [[Dalry, Ayrshire|Dalry]], [[Beith]] and [[Kilbirnie]], once a centre of [[steel]] and [[textile]] production long since gone. Tourism is the main industry on [[Isle of Arran|Arran]] and [[Cumbrae]], however the number of holiday homes on the latter has begun to squeeze locals out of the housing market. The seat has two [[nuclear power]] stations, [[Hunterston A nuclear power station]] (currently being decommissioned) and [[Hunterston B nuclear power station]], as well as a [[windfarm]] at Ardrossan. Kilwinning which is North Ayrshire's second largest town in the south of the constituency has many very affluent areas as well as working class areas but while the constituency maybe seen as working class as a whole, It is considered mixed due to the wealthy and working class areas within it

==History==
The constituency of [[North Ayrshire (UK Parliament constituency)|North Ayrshire]] was created in 1868 and was much larger than the present constituency, as it also included the towns of [[Irvine, North Ayrshire|Irvine]] and [[Kilmarnock]]. It returned various [[Liberal Party (UK)|Liberal]], [[Liberal Unionist Party|Liberal Unionist]] and [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] MP's, the most well known of whom being Sir [[Aylmer Hunter-Weston]], a general in the [[First World War]] who served as MP from 1916 to 1935 first for North Ayrshire and then for [[Bute and Northern Ayrshire (UK Parliament constituency)|Bute and Northern Ayrshire]] which was created in 1918. Another well-known MP was [[Sir Fitzroy Maclean, 1st Baronet|Sir Fitzroy Maclean]], a major-general in the [[Second World War]] who was rumoured to be one of Ian Fleming's inspirations for [[James Bond]].

In 1983, the [[Cunninghame North (UK Parliament constituency)|Cunninghame North]] constituency was created. From 1911 to 1987, the constituencies containing North Ayrshire returned either Conservative Party or Unionist Party MP's until the [[1987 United Kingdom general election|1987 general election]] when the constituency was lost to the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]]. In 2015, the seat was won by the [[Scottish National Party|SNP]]. In the [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017 general election]] the SNP held the seat with a much reduced majority, with the Conservatives more than doubling their vote share.

