'''Paisley and Renfrewshire South ''' is a [[United Kingdom constituencies|constituency]] of the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]], located in [[Renfrewshire]], [[Scotland]] to the southwest of [[Glasgow]]. It elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|member of Parliament]] at least once every five years using the [[First-past-the-post voting|first-past-the-post]] system of voting.

==Boundaries==
{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Paisley and Renfrewshire South (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame=yes|text=Map of current boundaries}}
This seat was created in 2005 from the bulk of the former [[Paisley South (UK Parliament constituency)|Paisley South]] seat, with minor additions from neighbouring constituencies.<ref name="aristotle">{{Cite news|work= [[The Guardian]] |access-date= 2008-05-28 |url= http://politics.guardian.co.uk/hoc/constituency/0,,-1214,00.html |title= Paisley and Renfrewshire South: Aristotle |location= London |url-status= dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080618083455/http://politics.guardian.co.uk/hoc/constituency/0,,-1214,00.html |archive-date= 18 June 2008 }}</ref> Covering the southern portion of the [[Renfrewshire]] council area, the constituency includes around half of [[Paisley, Renfrewshire|Paisley]], as well as the smaller town of [[Johnstone]] and the villages of [[Kilbarchan]] and [[Elderslie, Scotland|Elderslie]]. The reminder of the seat is more rural, containing the villages of [[Lochwinnoch]], [[Howwood]], several [[hamlets]] and farmland. The constituency also contains the [[Gleniffer Braes|Gleniffer Braes Country Park]] to the south and [[Clyde Muirshiel Regional Park]] to the west, notable for [[Castle Semple Loch]].<ref>[https://www.bcomm-scotland.independent.gov.uk/sites/default/files/2023_review_final/bcs_2023_review_report_web_version.pdf Boundary Commission Scotland 2023 Review Report]</ref>

Further to the completion of the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], the small town of [[Linwood, Renfrewshire|Linwood]] and village of [[Brookfield, Renfrewshire|Brookfield]] is to be included in the constituency, alongside some minor changes in Paisley. The redrawn seat is due to be contested for the first time at the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election.]]<ref>[https://www.bcomm-scotland.independent.gov.uk/sites/default/files/2023_review_final/bcs_2023_review_report_web_version.pdf Boundary Commission Scotland 2023 Review Report]</ref>

==Members of Parliament==
The constituency's first MP was [[Douglas Alexander]], who had held the seat since its creation in 2005 and its predecessor [[Paisley South (UK Parliament constituency)|Paisley South]] since 1997. Alexander was the Shadow [[Secretary of State for Foreign and Commonwealth Affairs|Foreign Secretary]], and has previously held Cabinet posts such as [[Secretary of State for Transport|Transport Secretary]] and [[Secretary of State for Scotland|Scottish Secretary]] (2006–07; joint), and [[Secretary of State for International Development|International Development Secretary]] (2007–10).

When SNP candidate [[Mhairi Black]] gained the seat in May 2015, she was 20 years and 237 days old, making her the youngest ever Member of Parliament (MP) elected to the House of Commons since at least the [[Reform Act of 1832]], replacing William Wentworth-Fitzwilliam; who was 20 years and 11 months old when elected in 1832.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Rix |first1=Kathryn |title=The youngest MP? The 'baby' of the first Reformed Parliament |url=https://victoriancommons.wordpress.com/2015/05/11/the-youngest-mp-the-baby-of-the-first-reformed-parliament/ |website=The Victorian Commons |language=en |date=11 May 2015}}</ref> She subsequently held the seat at the [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thenational.scot/news/15338087.mhairi-black-defies-exit-poll-odds-by-holding-paisley-renfrewshire-south/|title=Mhairi Black defies exit poll odds by holding Paisley & Renfrewshire South|website=The National|date=9 June 2017 }}</ref> and [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general elections]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.renfrewshire.gov.uk/article/9655/2019-UK-General-Election-Results|title = 2019 UK General Election Results}}</ref> On 4 July 2023, Black announced that she would be standing down as an MP at the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|next general election]].<ref>{{cite news |title= Mhairi Black to step down as SNP MP at next election |url= https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-scotland-66101123 |publisher=BBC News |date=4 July 2023 |accessdate=4 July 2023}}</ref>

