'''Glasgow South West''' is a [[United Kingdom constituencies|constituency]] of the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom]]. As in all other seats since the 1950 abolition of multi-member [[University constituency|university returns to the Commons]], residents elect one [[Member of Parliament (MP)]] by the [[First-past-the-post voting|first past the post]] system of election.

The seat saw its first MP at the [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005 general election]]. Its [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017 general election]] result was the ninth-closest result, with a winning margin of 60 votes.

At the [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general election]], [[Chris Stephens]] of the [[Scottish National Party|SNP]] was re-elected with an increased majority over Matt Kerr of [[Labour and Co-operative Party|Labour Co-op]], with 4,900 votes and a 7.2% swing from [[2017 United Kingdom general election|the previous election two years earlier]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/insights/ge2017-marginal-seats-and-turnout/|title=GE2017: Marginal seats and turnout|first=House of Commons|last=Library|date=23 June 2017}}</ref> However, in [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024]], he lost his seat to [[Zubir Ahmed]] of the [[Scottish Labour|Labour Party]] by 3,285 votes and a 9.2% swing to Labour.<ref name=glasgowsw24/>

==Boundaries==
{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Glasgow South West (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame=yes|frame-width=205|frame-height=250|text=Map of current boundaries}}
The Glasgow City wards of Cardonald, Crookston, Darnley, Drumoyne, Govan, Ibrox, Mosspark, Nitshill, North Cardonald, Penilee, and Pollok.

The seat is one of seven covering the [[Glasgow City council area]]; none have overspill.

Before the 2005 general election the city was covered by ten constituencies, of which two straddled boundaries with other council areas. The area's representatives before its inception were those for [[Glasgow Pollok (UK Parliament constituency)|Glasgow Pollok]] and to a lesser extent [[Glasgow Govan (UK Parliament constituency)|Glasgow Govan]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bcomm-scotland.gov.uk/5th%20report/index.htm |title=Fifth Periodical Review |website=[[Boundary Commission for Scotland]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071009160559fw_/http://www.bcomm-scotland.gov.uk/5th%20report/index.htm |archive-date=9 October 2007}}</ref>

[[Scottish Parliament constituencies]] retain the names and boundaries of the immediate forebear seats.

