'''Lothian East''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|l|oʊ|ð|i|ə|n}}; {{lang-sco|Lowden East}}; {{lang-gd|Lodainn an Ear}}) is a [[United Kingdom constituencies|constituency]] in [[Scotland]] which returns one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) to the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom]], elected by the [[First-past-the-post voting|first past the post]] voting system.

The constituency was formerly known as '''East Lothian'''. It was renamed as a result of the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]].<ref>[https://www.bcomm-scotland.independent.gov.uk/sites/default/files/2023_review_final/bcs_2023_review_report_web_version.pdf Boundary Commission Scotland 2023 Review Report]</ref> It is first being contested under the new name at the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]].

==Constituency profile==
The seat covers small towns to the east of Edinburgh including [[Haddington, East Lothian|Haddington]] and [[Dunbar]] which have good commuting links to the capital city; and a more rural area extending south into the [[Lammermuir Hills]]. Residents' health and wealth are around average for the UK.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Seat Details - East Lothian |url=https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/seatdetails.py?seat=East+Lothian |access-date=2024-06-04 |website=www.electoralcalculus.co.uk}}</ref>

==History==
[[File:EastLothianConstituency.svg|thumb|East Lothian from 2005-24]]
The constituency was created for the [[1983 United Kingdom general election|1983 general election]]. Until the SNP landslide victory in [[2015 United Kingdom general election|2015]], the seat had been continuously represented by MPs from the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] since the constituency's creation 32 years earlier. The East Lothian Constituency Labour Party voted on 22 January 2010 to [[Deselection of Labour MPs|deselect]] the previous MP [[Anne Moffat]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://news.scotsman.com/politics/Moffat-faces-deselection-after-police.6010307.jp|title=Moffat faces deselection after police called to club rebellion|date=25 January 2010|publisher=Edinburgh Evening News|access-date=30 January 2010}}</ref> The [[National Executive Committee of the Labour Party|National Executive Committee]] upheld the decision on 23 March 2010.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2010/mar/23/labour-upholds-anne-moffat-deselection |title=Labour party upholds decision to deselect East Lothian MP Anne Moffat |date=23 March 2010 |newspaper=The Guardian |access-date=23 March 2010 | location=London | first=Severin | last=Carrell}}</ref> Fiona O'Donnell was elected in [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010]] with an increased majority for Labour compared to [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005]]. O'Donnell lost her seat to George Kerevan of the SNP at the 2015 general election; who was elected with a majority of 6,803 votes.

From 2015 until the [[2017 United Kingdom general election|snap general election in 2017]], the constituency was represented by [[George Kerevan]] of the [[Scottish National Party]]; who was defeated by [[Martin Whitfield]] of the Labour Party by 3,083 votes. Two years later, at the [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general election]], Whitfield was defeated by former [[Scottish National Party]] MSP and Justice Secretary [[Kenny MacAskill]]. On 26 March 2021, MacAskill defected from the SNP to the [[Alba Party]].

At the 2014 Scottish independence referendum, a majority of voters nationwide opted for Scotland to remain a part of the United Kingdom - with 61.72% of the electorate of East Lothian voting for staying in the United Kingdom and 38.28% voting for independence.<ref>{{cite web |title=Scottish independence referendum - Results - BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/events/scotland-decides/results |website=BBC News |access-date=7 July 2021}}</ref>

== Boundaries ==
=== East Lothian ===
'''1983–1997''': East Lothian District.

'''1997–2005''': The East Lothian District electoral divisions of Fa'side, Haddington, Luffness, Preston/Levenhall, and Tantallon.

'''2005–2024''': East Lothian Council area.

=== Lothian East ===
'''2024–present''': East Lothian Council area, except for most of the [[Musselburgh (ward)|Musselburgh ward]].

Before the 1983 general election, the area lay in the [[Berwick and East Lothian (UK Parliament constituency)|Berwick and East Lothian]] constituency.

