'''Airdrie and Shotts''' is a [[United Kingdom constituencies|constituency]] of the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|UK House of Commons]], located in central [[Scotland]] within the [[North Lanarkshire]] council area. It elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) at least once every five years using the [[first-past-the-post]] system of voting.

The constituency has existed since 1997; however, it underwent significant boundary changes in 2005. Before 2015, it could have been described as a [[safe seat]] for the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]], who held it with a majority of over 12,000 votes until [[Neil Gray]] of the [[Scottish National Party]] (SNP) was elected at [[2015 United Kingdom general election|that year's general election]]. Former MPs for the constituency include: [[Pamela Nash]], former [[Baby of the House]], [[John Reid, Baron Reid of Cardowan|John Reid]], former Labour [[Home Secretary]] and [[Secretary of State for Defence|Defence Secretary]], and [[Helen Liddell, Baroness Liddell of Coatdyke|Helen Liddell]], former Labour [[Secretary of State for Scotland|Scottish Secretary]].

It is a generally working-class, urban seat, and contains the towns of [[Airdrie, North Lanarkshire|Airdrie]], [[Calderbank]], [[Chapelhall]], [[Glenmavis]] and [[Shotts]].

From 2015-2021, the Member of Parliament for the seat was Neil Gray of the SNP. He resigned in March 2021 to run for a seat in the [[Scottish Parliament]], triggering [[2021 Airdrie and Shotts by-election|a by-election]], which was won by [[Anum Qaisar]].<ref>{{cite tweet|user=BBCPhilipSim|last=Sim|first=Philip|number=1374763771273617412|title=This is now confirmed: Neil Gray has gone from being MP for Airdrie and Shotts to the Steward and Bailiff of the Manor of Northstead (...which is one way of quitting Westminster) - and as of tomorrow will be the SNP candidate to be MSP for Airdrie and Shotts. By-election incoming|access-date=28 March 2021}}</ref> The seat was re-gained by Labour at the [[2024 United Kingdom general election in Scotland|2024 general election]] and is currently represented by [[Kenneth Stevenson (politician)|Kenneth Stevenson]].

==Constituency profile==
The majority of this constituency maintains the boundaries of its predecessor. From the [[North Lanarkshire (UK Parliament constituency)|North Lanarkshire constituency]] come [[Holytown]] and [[Newarthill]]. This seat is positioned on either side of the [[M8 motorway (Scotland)|M8 motorway]] to [[Glasgow]]. Small semi-urban towns such as Shotts (including Stane and Dykehead) were added to [[Airdrie, North Lanarkshire|Airdrie]] in the 1997 redistribution to form this seat, the predecessor of which was [[Monklands East (UK Parliament constituency)|Monklands East]]. An area in the eastern part of [[Coatbridge]] is also part of the constituency.

[[Electoral Calculus]] describes the seat as "Traditional", characterised by working class people with lower levels of income and formal education.<ref>{{cite web|publisher=Electoral Calculus|title=Airdrie and Shotts: Seat Details|url=https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/seatdetails.py?seat=Airdrie+and+Shotts|accessdate=6 October 2023}}</ref>

== Boundaries and history ==
{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Airdrie and Shotts (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame=yes|frame-width=200|text=Map of boundaries from 2024}}
'''1997–2005''': The [[Monklands (district)|Monklands District]] electoral divisions of Airdrie North and Airdrie South, and the [[Motherwell (district)|Motherwell District]] electoral division of Fortissat.

'''2005–2024''': The [[North Lanarkshire Council]] wards of academy, Airdrie Central, Airdrie North,<ref name="auto">{{cite web |url=https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/cgi-bin/seatdetails.py?seat=Airdrie%20and%20Shotts |title=Seat Details |last=Baxter |first=Martin |date=14 September 2018 |publisher=Electoral Calculus|access-date=8 February 2019 }}{{deadlink|date=October 2023}}</ref> Airdrie South,<ref name="auto"/> Benhar, Calderbank, Chapelhall, Clarkston, Craigneuk and Petersburn, Dykehead, Holytown, New Monkland West, Newarthill, Newmains, North Cairnhill and Coatdyke, Plains and Caldercruix, Salsburgh, South East Cairnhill and Gartlea, Stane, and Whinhall. In 2007 these were converted into multi-member wards, with the following falling within the constituency: [[Airdrie Central (ward)|Airdrie Central]], [[Airdrie North (ward)|Airdrie North]], [[Airdrie South (ward)|Airdrie South]], [[Fortissat (ward)|Fortissat]], [[Mossend and Holytown (ward)|Mossend and Holytown]] (part), [[Murdostoun (ward)|Murdostoun]] (part).

The 2005 changes saw the seat lose some territory to [[Motherwell and Wishaw (UK Parliament constituency)|Motherwell and Wishaw]] whilst gaining part of [[Hamilton North and Bellshill (UK Parliament constituency)|Hamilton North and Bellshill]]. It proved to be a [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] stronghold from its creation until the [[Scottish National Party]] gained it at the 2015 general election, with [[Neil Gray]] winning the seat from [[Pamela Nash]] by 8,779 votes. Gray's majority dipped to just 195 in the 2017 election. However, in the 2019 election he won by 5,201 votes.<ref name=BBC2019>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/politics/constituencies/S14000003|title=Airdrie & Shotts parliamentary constituency - Election 2019|access-date=16 December 2019|publisher=BBC News}}</ref>

On 24 March 2021 it was formally announced that [[Neil Gray]] had officially stood down as the Member of Parliament for Airdrie and Shotts to contest the [[2021 Scottish Parliament election]] seat of the [[Airdrie and Shotts (Scottish Parliament constituency)|same name]].

Following the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]] the constituency was redrawn with minor changes before [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]].

