'''Dumfries and Galloway''' is a [[county constituency]] in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom]]. It was first used in the [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005 general election]], and replaced [[Galloway and Upper Nithsdale (UK Parliament constituency)|Galloway and Upper Nithsdale]] and part of [[Dumfriesshire (UK Parliament constituency)|Dumfries.]] It elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) by the [[first past the post]] system of election. Despite its name, it does not cover the whole of the [[Dumfries and Galloway]] council area.

==Constituency profile==
Located in the southwest of Scotland, this is a large and rural seat with significant farming and forestry sectors, including the [[Galloway Forest Park]]. Dumfries is an economic hub for south Scotland and is also Dumfries and Galloway's largest town. The seat also contains many other small towns and villages such as Castle Douglas, Dalbeattie, Auchincairn, Kirkcudbright, Gatehouse of Fleet, Creetown, Glenluce, and Stranraer. Stranraer, which is the area's second-largest town, was formerly a port town where ferries to Northern Ireland departed from, but they have since moved to Cairnryan, six miles north of Stranraer. The seat has had a mixed electoral history. In [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005]], the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] won the seat and held it again in [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010]], but lost the seat to the [[Scottish National Party]] (SNP) in [[2015 United Kingdom general election|2015]]. The SNP held the seat from 2015 until 2017 before losing the seat to the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] in the [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017 general election]], who held it in [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019]].

== Boundaries ==
The Dumfries and Galloway constituency was created by the Fifth Review of the [[Boundary Commission for Scotland]], and covers part of the [[Dumfries and Galloway]] council area. The rest of the council area is covered by the [[Dumfriesshire, Clydesdale and Tweeddale (UK Parliament constituency)|Dumfriesshire, Clydesdale and Tweeddale]] constituency, which also covers part of the Scottish Borders council area and part of the South Lanarkshire council area.

'''2005-2024''': The Dumfries and Galloway Council wards of Stranraer and the Rhins, Abbey, Castle Douglas & Crocketford, Dee and Glenkens, Lochar (part), Mid Galloway and Wigtown West, Mid & Upper Nithsdale (part), Nith (part), and North West Dumfries.

'''2024-present''': As above with some boundary changes within the Lochar and Nith wards.

==Political history==
Dumfries and Galloway's predecessor seats, [[Galloway and Upper Nithsdale (UK Parliament constituency)|Galloway and Upper Nithsdale]] (1983–2005) and [[Galloway (UK Parliament constituency)|Galloway]] (1918–83), had been represented by [[Scottish Conservatives|Conservative]] MPs in all but two parliaments since 1931. Galloway and Upper Nithsdale was won by the [[Scottish National Party]] in 1997<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url = http://news.bbc.co.uk/hi/english/static/vote2001/results_constituencies/constituencies/261.stm|title = Vote 2001: Results & Constituencies: Galloway & Upper Nithsdale|access-date = 2015-05-09|website = BBC News|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20141015001730/http://news.bbc.co.uk/hi/english/static/vote2001/results_constituencies/constituencies/261.stm|archive-date = 2014-10-15}}</ref> but became the only Scottish seat to return a Conservative MP at the [[2001 United Kingdom general election|2001 general election]].<ref name=":0" />

Boundary changes for the [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005 election]] saw the new seat have a very slim [[Scottish Labour Party|Labour]] majority over the Conservatives, and the SNP were in close third place. [[Russell Brown (Scottish politician)|Russell Brown]] was the Labour candidate, who had been the MP for the neighbouring seat of [[Dumfriesshire (UK Parliament constituency)|Dumfriesshire]] since [[1997 United Kingdom general election|1997]], and [[Peter Duncan (Scottish politician)|Peter Duncan]], the sitting MP for Galloway and Upper Nithsdale, stood as the Conservative candidate. Although the seat was the Conservatives' second target seat across Britain,<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/shared/vote2005/html/684.stm|title = Election 2005: Result: Dumfries and Galloway|date = 2005-05-06|access-date = 2015-05-09|work =BBC News |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20141012074204/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/shared/vote2005/html/684.stm|archive-date =2014-10-12}}</ref> Labour increased its vote share and Russell Brown was elected as the constituency's MP.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|url = http://www.dumgal.gov.uk/CHttpHandler.ashx?id=2500&p=0|title = General Election - Dumfries and Galloway County Constituency - May 2005|date = 2005-05-05|access-date = 2015-05-09|website = dumgal.gov.uk|last = Jones|first = Philip N.|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150509123540/http://www.dumgal.gov.uk/CHttpHandler.ashx?id=2500&p=0|archive-date = 2015-05-09}}
*{{Cite web|url = http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/shared/vote2005/html/684.stm|title = Election 2005: Result: Dumfries and Galloway|date = 2005-05-06|access-date = 2015-05-09|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20141012074204/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/shared/vote2005/html/684.stm|archive-date =2014-10-12}}</ref>

In [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010]], Duncan attempted once again to become Dumfries and Galloway's MP. However the election produced a swing against the Conservatives in the seat, and it was held by Labour's Russell Brown with a majority of 7,449 votes.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|url = http://www.dumgal.gov.uk/CHttpHandler.ashx?id=4603&p=0|title = General Election - Dumfries and Galloway Constituency - May 2010|date = 2010-05-07|access-date = 2015-05-09|website = dumgal.gov.uk|last = Haswell|first = Alex|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150509123449/http://www.dumgal.gov.uk/CHttpHandler.ashx?id=4603&p=0|archive-date = 2015-05-09}}
*{{Cite web|url = http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/shared/election2010/results/constituency/813.stm|title = Election 2010: Dumfries & Galloway|access-date = 2015-05-09|website = BBC News|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20141102225044/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/shared/election2010/results/constituency/813.stm|archive-date = 2014-11-02}}</ref> The SNP's share of the vote in the constituency collapsed at the 2005 general election, and remained static in 2010. In [[2015 United Kingdom general election|2015]], the seat was won by the SNP's [[Richard Arkless]] with a 6,514 vote majority. The Conservative share of the vote stayed similar to the 2010 election, whereas Labour polled third, receiving 24.7% of the vote compared to 45.9% in 2010.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web|url = http://dumgal.gov.uk/index.aspx?articleid=14586|title = UK Parliamentary Elections Results 2015 for the Dumfries and Galloway County|date = 2015-05-08|access-date = 2015-05-09|website = dumgal.gov.uk|last = Haswell|first = Alex|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150509122702/http://dumgal.gov.uk/index.aspx?articleid=14586|archive-date = 2015-05-09|url-status = dead}}
*{{Cite news|url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/politics/constituencies/S14000013|title = Election 2015: Dumfries & Galloway Parliamentary constituency|access-date = 2015-05-09|website = BBC News|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150509124451/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/politics/constituencies/S14000013|archive-date = 2015-05-09}}</ref><ref name=":2" /> In [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017]], [[Alister Jack]] gained the seat for the Conservatives,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.gallowaygazette.co.uk/news/politics/conservatives-take-dumfries-and-galloway-seat-from-snp-1-4470840|title=Conservatives take Dumfries and Galloway seat from SNP|website=www.gallowaygazette.co.uk|language=en|access-date=2019-09-14}}</ref> making him one of a dozen new Scottish Conservative MPs.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.heraldscotland.com/news/15470080.scottish-tory-mp-has-over-70000-of-shares-in-firm-in-worlds-worst-tax-haven/|title=Scottish Tory MP has over £70,000 of shares in firm in world's 'worst' tax haven|website=HeraldScotland|date=13 August 2017 }}</ref> Jack held the seat in [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019]] with a reduced majority despite increasing his vote share.

